VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-a11y
FAMILY: anthropic
TASK: W3
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Scope: git diff master -- web/templates/components/kpis.html (fix/phase-visibility, W3).
Audited in a throwaway copy of /home/darrell/bin/ai/budget2 (cp -a, .git kept). Rendered
the actual dashboard KPI handler (existing harness: internal/handlers/dashboard/
handlers_http_test.go TestDashboardKPIs_TargetProvenance_*) with two temporary dump tests
to capture ground-truth HTML; no project file was edited in the real tree.

Diff under test:
  <p class="text-sm ...">
-     Target <span class="num">{{formatMoney .Metrics.BudgetTarget}}</span> · ...
+     <span{{if $prov.Annotate}} title="{{$targetAnnotation}}" aria-label="{{$targetAnnotation}}"{{end}}>
+         Target <span class="num">{{formatMoney .Metrics.BudgetTarget}}</span>
+     </span> · ...
  where $targetAnnotation = "Target from What-If plan: {formatDollars Base} base ×
  {formatMultiplier Multiplier} ({PhaseName} phase)" (or the Straddles variant).

CHECK 1 — aria-label vs visible content (2.5.3 / 1.3.1 / 4.1.2): FAIL.
Rendered output, phase-active fixture (base $2,000/mo, Active phase, multiplier 0.95,
route /dashboard/kpis?start=2025-01-01&end=2025-01-31):

  <span title="Target from What-If plan: $2,000 base × 0.95 (Active phase)"
        aria-label="Target from What-If plan: $2,000 base × 0.95 (Active phase)">
    Target <span class="num">$1,900.00</span>
  </span> · <span class="num">$427.22</span>/mo under

aria-label on a non-interactive <span> overrides the accessible-name computation for
its entire subtree (WAI-ARIA accname spec; this is also why ARIA APG explicitly warns
against aria-label on plain containers). The value actually shown on screen — the
computed, phase-adjusted target "$1,900.00" — is Metrics.BudgetTarget, which is NOT the
same number as $prov.Base ("$2,000", pre-multiplier, and formatted with formatDollars
i.e. no cents, vs. formatMoney's cents). The aria-label string contains neither
"$1,900.00" nor any equivalent of it. A screen-reader user therefore hears "Target from
What-If plan: $2,000 base times 0.95, Active phase" and never hears the actual target
figure that sighted users read directly off the card — the single most important number
in this UI region is silently dropped for AT users. This is a content loss, not just a
naming mismatch: 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships) is violated because the
programmatically-determined text does not carry information present in the visual
presentation, and 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value) is violated because the exposed
"name"/text substitutes for, rather than supplements, the rendered value. (2.5.3 Label
in Name applies at most by analogy, since this span is not itself an interactive
control — see Check 3 — but the underlying defect it's meant to catch, an accessible
name that omits the visible text, is exactly what's happening here.)
Confirmed by rendering, not just template reading, via a temporary local test
(`go test ./internal/handlers/dashboard/... -run TestA11yDumpBody`) that hit the same
route as the project's own TestDashboardKPIs_TargetProvenance_AnnotatesWhenPhaseActive
(handlers_http_test.go:1521), which only asserts the title/aria-label strings exist —
it never asserts on the figure that AT users lose, so the existing test suite would not
catch this regression.
Fix direction (not prescribing implementation): the aria-label must restate the visible
target figure, e.g. aria-label="Target $1,900.00 from What-If plan: $2,000 base × 0.95
(Active phase)", or drop aria-label entirely and let AT read the natural content plus
title/aria-describedby for the extra provenance without suppressing the child text.

CHECK 2 — title is not the sole channel (1.4.13 / 2.1.1): PASS.
title and aria-label carry byte-identical strings (see rendered output above and the
diff: both interpolate the same {{$targetAnnotation}}). The provenance text is
therefore not title-only; it is exposed to AT via aria-label. (The FAIL in Check 1 is
about aria-label's content being wrong/incomplete, not about it being absent.)
OBSERVATION (non-blocking): since the span is non-focusable (Check 3), the native
title tooltip only ever appears on mouse hover, never on keyboard focus, so
sighted keyboard-only users never see the provenance breakdown at all. This is not a
new AA violation (1.4.13 governs content that DOES appear on hover-or-focus; it does
not mandate parity between the two triggers), but it's worth noting for the lead: the
provenance is effectively mouse-only for sighted users and screen-reader-only for AT
users, with keyboard-sighted users getting neither.

CHECK 3 — span not focusable: PASS.
Diff and rendered markup both show only conditional `title=` / `aria-label=` attributes
added; no `tabindex` was introduced. A non-interactive <span> with aria-label but no
tabindex/role is correctly not in the tab order, so there's no separate "focusable
non-interactive element" violation.

CHECK 4 — annotation genuinely absent in the no-phase case: PASS.
Ledger: {{if $prov.Annotate}}...{{end}} wraps the entire attribute block, so when
Annotate is false, the <span> emits no attributes at all (not empty-string title/
aria-label). Confirmed by rendering the no-phase-config fixture
(TestDashboardKPIs_TargetProvenance_AbsentWhenNoPhaseConfig's setup,
monthly_living_expenses only, no spending_phase_config):

  <span>Target <span class="num">$2,000.00</span></span> · <span class="num">$527.22</span>/mo under

No stray title="" / aria-label="" present; matches the project's own byte-identical-
rendering assertion in that test.

ACCESSIBILITY.md cross-check: no point in the repo's numbered standard names this
exact pattern verbatim, but point 4 ("aria-label where a visible label is genuinely
absent by design") and point 13 ("currency amounts include sign semantics in
accessible text where sign carries meaning", i.e. augment, don't replace, the visible
figure) both encode the same principle this diff violates: aria-label is meant to
supply what's visually missing or add semantic nuance, not to erase a rendered
currency figure. Treating this as a WCAG-grounded FAIL per Check 1's citations
(1.3.1 / 4.1.2, with 2.5.3's underlying concern applicable by extension).

VERDICT: FAIL — Check 1 is a genuine AA regression: the phase-adjusted target dollar
figure, visible to sighted users, is unreachable by screen-reader users because
aria-label replaces it with provenance text that omits it. Checks 2–4 pass as
specified.
